1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,615
On the night of the ninth of May 2025, during Operation Sindoor,

2
00:00:04,615 --> 00:00:10,103
drones and missiles came at Indian military and civilian areas from several directions at once.

3
00:00:10,103 --> 00:00:15,719
What happened over the next few hours was not really a battle between a missile and a drone.

4
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:22,425
It was a race against a clock.

5
00:00:22,425 --> 00:00:25,604
And afterwards, India gave its account of that night.

6
00:00:25,604 --> 00:00:33,316
The Press Information Bureau said that an Indian system called Akashteer had intercepted and neutralised every inbound projectile,

7
00:00:33,316 --> 00:00:39,680
and that Pakistan's imported HQ-9 and HQ-16 systems had failed to detect and intercept Indian strikes.

8
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:48,102
That is India's official account, published by the government; independent verification of the numbers is not public.

9
00:00:48,102 --> 00:00:50,454
But here is the part worth sitting with.

10
00:00:50,454 --> 00:00:52,060
Akashteer is not a missile.

11
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:53,966
It does not shoot anything.

12
00:00:58,067 --> 00:01:00,428
So let's do the simple version first.

13
00:01:00,428 --> 00:01:03,783
An air defence system does three jobs, in this order.

14
00:01:03,783 --> 00:01:05,771
It has to see something coming.

15
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:08,132
It has to decide what to do about it.

16
00:01:08,132 --> 00:01:09,748
And then it has to shoot.

17
00:01:09,748 --> 00:01:16,023
Most of us only ever think about the third one, because the third one is the one with the fireworks.

18
00:01:16,023 --> 00:01:17,639
The first two are harder.

19
00:01:17,639 --> 00:01:18,509
Second thing.

20
00:01:18,509 --> 00:01:20,176
Forget the word dome.

21
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,144
People say "an air defence dome", and it makes you picture a single magic bubble.

22
00:01:28,144 --> 00:01:30,073
That is not what anyone builds.

23
00:01:30,073 --> 00:01:33,450
What you build is a set of rings, one inside the other.

24
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:38,093
The outermost ring carries a long-range system with very expensive missiles.

25
00:01:38,093 --> 00:01:39,781
Inside it is a medium ring.

26
00:01:39,781 --> 00:01:41,770
Inside that, a short-range ring.

27
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:46,834
And right over the thing you are protecting, guns and shoulder-fired missiles.

28
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,876
Why go to all that trouble instead of just buying the best long-range system?

29
00:01:52,876 --> 00:01:53,605
Two reasons.

30
00:01:53,605 --> 00:01:57,252
Anything the outer ring misses, the next ring in gets a turn at.

31
00:01:57,252 --> 00:01:57,813
And cost.

32
00:01:57,813 --> 00:02:02,020
You do not fire a missile worth crores at a drone worth a couple of lakhs.

33
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:06,303
Each ring exists so the ring outside it does not have to waste itself.

34
00:02:15,767 --> 00:02:17,078
Four questions, then.

35
00:02:17,078 --> 00:02:20,060
What is the S-400 India bought, and why that one?

36
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:22,326
What is India building to replace it?

37
00:02:22,326 --> 00:02:26,380
Why is stopping a ballistic missile a completely different problem?

38
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:30,079
And what is Mission Sudarshan Chakra actually promising?

39
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:33,230
Start with what exists.

40
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:36,162
The outer ring is the S-400, a Russian system.

41
00:02:36,162 --> 00:02:42,461
Inside it sits MR-SAM, a medium-range missile built jointly by DRDO and Israel Aerospace Industries,

42
00:02:42,461 --> 00:02:48,437
used by both the Army and the Air Force, with a published range around seventy kilometres.

43
00:02:52,667 --> 00:02:58,924
Then the short-range ring: Akash, which is Indian, with its newer Akash Prime version; the Israeli SPYDER;

44
00:02:58,924 --> 00:03:05,123
and QRSAM, a quick-reaction Indian system designed to move with an armoured column and shoot on the move.

45
00:03:05,123 --> 00:03:10,093
And then the innermost ring, within a few kilometres of whatever is being protected:

46
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:14,487
anti-aircraft guns, and shoulder-fired missiles carried by a soldier.

47
00:03:19,133 --> 00:03:21,166
Now hold on to one name there.

48
00:03:21,166 --> 00:03:21,625
Akash.

49
00:03:21,625 --> 00:03:27,460
Remember the five missiles of the nineteen eighty-three programme from our last episode?

50
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:29,820
Prithvi, Trishul, Akash, Nag, Agni?

51
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:30,607
That Akash.

52
00:03:30,607 --> 00:03:37,295
A missile sketched out in the nineteen eighties is, forty years later, a working layer of the shield.

53
00:03:37,295 --> 00:03:39,759
These programmes take that long.

54
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:43,225
The S-400.

55
00:03:43,225 --> 00:03:47,378
India signed for it on the fifth of October 2018: five squadrons,

56
00:03:47,378 --> 00:03:52,788
for about five point four three billion dollars, roughly forty thousand crore rupees.

57
00:03:52,788 --> 00:04:00,401
The Air Force calls it Sudarshan Chakra, and hold on to that name too, because it comes back at the end of this episode.

58
00:04:00,401 --> 00:04:03,217
What you are buying is not one missile.

59
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:11,021
It is a package: a search radar that scans a wide area, a second radar that locks and guides,

60
00:04:11,021 --> 00:04:16,242
a command vehicle, and launchers carrying several different missiles for different distances.

61
00:04:16,242 --> 00:04:21,741
With its longest-reaching missile, published figures put it out to around four hundred kilometres.

62
00:04:21,741 --> 00:04:26,874
It can track a large number of targets at once and engage many of them simultaneously.

63
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,862
That last part is what makes it a system rather than a weapon.

64
00:04:37,862 --> 00:04:44,759
India bought it despite a real risk of American sanctions under a law called CAATSA, which were never ultimately imposed.

65
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:48,321
And then deliveries ran late — the original schedule was 2023,

66
00:04:48,321 --> 00:04:52,165
and as of the seventeenth of November 2026 the Defence Ministry has

67
00:04:52,165 --> 00:04:56,423
not announced that the fifth and final squadron has been handed over.

68
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:02,803
And that delay is the whole argument in one.

69
00:05:02,803 --> 00:05:08,478
When the best layer of your shield is made in another country, three things are outside your control.

70
00:05:08,478 --> 00:05:09,424
When it arrives.

71
00:05:09,424 --> 00:05:11,260
Whether spare parts keep coming.

72
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:17,123
And what happens to that factory if the country making it is at war, or sanctioned, or simply busy.

73
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:24,050
None of that is a criticism of the system.

74
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:30,777
The S-400 is a capable piece of equipment, and officials have said publicly that it performed well in May 2025.

75
00:05:30,777 --> 00:05:32,939
It is an argument about dependence.

76
00:05:32,939 --> 00:05:38,225
Which brings us to the thing India has been building quietly for most of a decade.

77
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,688
It is called Project Kusha.

78
00:05:43,688 --> 00:05:48,935
It is DRDO's long-range surface-to-air missile — India's own answer to the outer ring.

79
00:05:48,935 --> 00:05:53,216
The Cabinet Committee on Security cleared its development in May 2022.

80
00:05:53,216 --> 00:05:57,679
In September 2023 the Defence Ministry approved procurement of five squad

81
00:05:57,679 --> 00:06:02,441
rons for the Air Force at twenty-one thousand seven hundred crore rupees.

82
00:06:05,133 --> 00:06:08,366
And the design is interesting, because it is not one missile.

83
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:15,352
It is three interceptors sharing one set of radars and one command system: a short one reaching about a hundred and fifty kilometres,

84
00:06:15,352 --> 00:06:21,596
a middle one at around two hundred and fifty, and a long one reported at three hundred and fifty to four hundred.

85
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:33,548
Then, on the twenty-third of July 2026, Kusha flew for the first time, from Dr APJ Abdul Kalam Island off the Odisha coast.

86
00:06:33,548 --> 00:06:39,915
The Defence Ministry said the missile successfully intercepted a high-speed, high-altitude aerial target.

87
00:06:39,915 --> 00:06:42,138
The Defence Minister, Rajnath Singh,

88
00:06:42,138 --> 00:06:49,706
said that the capability to develop long-range surface-to-air missile systems is possessed only by a handful of nations.

89
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:55,150
Now, one successful first flight is a beginning, not a system.

90
00:06:55,150 --> 00:06:59,320
There are years of testing between that and a squadron standing on alert.

91
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:03,264
Officials have talked about induction towards the end of this decade.

92
00:07:03,264 --> 00:07:09,594
But the significance is real: the outer ring is the layer India could not build, and now it has flown one.

93
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,555
Now a distinction that gets blurred constantly.

94
00:07:15,555 --> 00:07:18,985
Shooting down an aircraft or a cruise missile is one problem.

95
00:07:18,985 --> 00:07:22,580
Shooting down a ballistic missile is another problem altogether.

96
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:26,120
An airliner travels at roughly nine hundred kilometres an hour.

97
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:31,762
A ballistic warhead coming back down through the atmosphere is doing several kilometres every second.

98
00:07:31,762 --> 00:07:37,372
You are not tracking it; you are predicting where it will be and putting something there first.

99
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:44,586
India has a separate programme for exactly this, sanctioned back in 2000.

100
00:07:44,586 --> 00:07:49,621
Its first phase was designed against missiles of up to the two thousand kilometre class,

101
00:07:49,621 --> 00:07:56,693
using two interceptors and a long-range tracking radar, and the government has said that phase has been tested and deployed.

102
00:07:56,693 --> 00:08:02,293
A second phase, aimed at the five thousand kilometre class, has been flight-tested from Chandipur.

103
00:08:02,293 --> 00:08:07,685
What is publicly known about where it is deployed is very little, and that is deliberate.

104
00:08:11,933 --> 00:08:16,490
Which brings us back to where we started, and to the thing almost nobody names.

105
00:08:16,490 --> 00:08:24,236
Every weapon in every ring is useless on its own, because the hard question in air defence is not "can this missile reach that target".

106
00:08:24,236 --> 00:08:31,925
It is: of the dozen weapons that can reach it, which one should fire, and have we already shot at it twice, and is that aircraft ours?

107
00:08:31,925 --> 00:08:33,819
You have seconds to answer.

108
00:08:38,167 --> 00:08:39,798
So India built a network.

109
00:08:39,798 --> 00:08:45,384
The Air Force runs a system called IACCS, the Integrated Air Command and Control System.

110
00:08:45,384 --> 00:08:46,953
The Army runs Akashteer.

111
00:08:46,953 --> 00:08:51,660
The Navy runs TRIGUN. They connect, and they share one picture of the sky.

112
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:58,124
The PIB describes Akashteer as a fully indigenous, automated air defence control and reporting system,

113
00:08:58,124 --> 00:09:06,143
mounted on vehicles so it can move, pulling in several kinds of radar, and giving everyone a common real-time air picture.

114
00:09:15,067 --> 00:09:22,028
The reason that matters is unglamorous and enormous: it stops two units firing at the same drone while a third goes unengaged,

115
00:09:22,028 --> 00:09:25,318
and it reduces the chance of shooting at your own aircraft.

116
00:09:25,318 --> 00:09:27,839
A better missile makes one engagement better.

117
00:09:27,839 --> 00:09:30,770
A better network makes every engagement better.

118
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:43,493
And on the fifteenth of August 2025, from the Red Fort, the Prime Minister announced the ambition that ties all of this together:

119
00:09:43,493 --> 00:09:48,782
Mission Sudarshan Chakra, a national security shield to be built out and modernised by 2035.

120
00:09:48,782 --> 00:09:54,526
He said the protective shield must keep expanding so that every citizen of the country feels secure,

121
00:09:54,526 --> 00:10:00,001
and that whatever technology may be used to attack us, our technology must prove superior.

122
00:10:05,467 --> 00:10:08,318
Two things about it are worth being precise on.

123
00:10:08,318 --> 00:10:14,437
First, it is described as both defensive and offensive — not only stopping an attack but answering it.

124
00:10:14,437 --> 00:10:20,378
Second, it is explicitly meant to cover civilian sites too: hospitals, railways, places of worship.

125
00:10:20,378 --> 00:10:23,348
Beyond that, the details have not been published,

126
00:10:23,348 --> 00:10:30,124
and anything you read about exactly which systems it will include is informed speculation, not announcement.

127
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:37,602
This is where thoughtful people disagree, and both sides are worth hearing.

128
00:10:37,602 --> 00:10:43,408
The case for: May 2025 showed that layered air defence works, that a cheap drone attack can be absorbed,

129
00:10:43,408 --> 00:10:49,183
and that the right lesson is to build more of it at home so it cannot be switched off from abroad.

130
00:10:53,767 --> 00:10:56,783
The case for caution: a shield invites bigger salvos,

131
00:10:56,783 --> 00:11:04,380
because the cheapest way past any defence is to send more than it can handle at once — and drones are cheap while interceptors are not,

132
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:07,361
so the arithmetic can run against the defender.

133
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:18,927
Some analysts add that visible defences can look destabilising to a neighbour, who may build more offence in response.

134
00:11:18,927 --> 00:11:22,715
Notice that neither argument is about whether the equipment works.

135
00:11:22,715 --> 00:11:25,954
They are arguments about what other people do next.

136
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:34,078
So, as of the seventeenth of November 2026.

137
00:11:34,078 --> 00:11:41,729
The S-400: contracted for five squadrons, deliveries behind the original schedule, no official word on the last one.

138
00:11:41,729 --> 00:11:47,589
Project Kusha: first flight done, induction expected towards the end of this decade.

139
00:11:49,233 --> 00:11:52,899
Akash and its family, MR-SAM, QRSAM: in service.

140
00:11:52,899 --> 00:11:59,182
Akashteer: fielded, and the government credits it with holding the sky in May 2025.

141
00:11:59,182 --> 00:12:06,214
And Mission Sudarshan Chakra: a stated goal, with a date on it, and not much else public.

142
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:14,223
If you remember one thing from this episode, make it the order of the three jobs,

143
00:12:14,223 --> 00:12:20,516
because it is also the order of difficulty, and it is backwards from how everyone talks about air defence.

144
00:12:20,516 --> 00:12:20,810
See.

145
00:12:20,810 --> 00:12:21,280
Decide.

146
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:21,692
Shoot.

147
00:12:21,692 --> 00:12:23,757
The missile is the easy part.

148
00:12:28,067 --> 00:12:29,808
Four cards on the wall now.

149
00:12:29,808 --> 00:12:34,844
Two missiles that strike, one that deters, and the thing that stops all of them.

150
00:12:34,844 --> 00:12:40,813
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: Tejas, and why building a fighter jet took India forty years.

151
00:12:40,813 --> 00:12:44,658
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

